<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/11th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Manzikert</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1071">1071</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Manzikert · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-seljuk-wars">Byzantine–Seljuk wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Manzikert was fought in 1071 in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> under Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanos-iv-diogenes">Romanos IV Diogenes</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alp-arslan">Alp Arslan</a>. The Byzantine army was defeated and the emperor captured. The battle opened Anatolia to Turkish settlement, a lasting loss for the Byzantine Empire and a foundational moment in the westward expansion associated with the Seljuk Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert.jpg" alt="Battle of Manzikert" width="320" height="313" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rohan Master · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:131_Bataille_de_Malazgirt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>More followed the Byzantine annexation of Greater Armenia in 1045 and its conquest by the Seljuk Turks nineteen years later: after <b>Manzikert</b> in 1071 Malik-Shah's governors laid repressive taxes on the Armenians, who fled west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The decisive blow to Byzantine military power came at the <b>Battle of Manzikert</b> in 1071, where the Seljuk Turks under Alp Arslan defeated and captured Emperor Romanos IV Diogenes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…remnant passed to the Attalids of Pergamon in 188 BCE and to Rome in 133 BCE, and the land was divided and redivided as Roman and Byzantine provinces until the Turks overran it after the <b>Battle of Manzikert</b> in 1071 and took complete control in the 13th century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-phrygia">Kingdom of Phrygia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The empire's most consequential engagement was the <b>Battle of Manzikert</b> in August 1071, where Sultan Alp Arslan defeated the Byzantine emperor Romanos IV Diogenes and captured him alive, the first time a Byzantine emperor had been taken prisoner by a Muslim ruler; the defeat and the Byzantine civil…</blockquote><a href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Byzantine defeat at the <b>Battle of Manzikert</b> in 1071, Turkmen bands spread across Anatolia, and rival Byzantine claimants hired them as allies in their civil wars.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Near <b>Manzikert</b> in eastern Anatolia, the Seljuk sultan Alp Arslan defeated the Byzantine army and captured Emperor Romanos IV Diogenes.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-26">26 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
